r have no common factors, so the expression can not be reduced. The expression \n\n$$\n\\frac{y+7}{2}\n$$\n\n is already in lowest terms."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544857 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105742 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbc7c6e8-63c7-5ac4-9b65-f12c006b3fde" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Power of Quotient Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0488e644-10d3-5995-89fe-a39cddb4f715" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14÷9\\times 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544096} {:answer/id #uuid "65e4993a-02e0-521d-8723-fa58f78166db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14÷{9}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544307} {:answer/id #uuid "67465fb6-9c4a-5dac-aa63-96db2d3a8194" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{14}^{4}÷9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544311} {:answer/id #uuid "cf4fe88c-5180-59e4-92ef-ead88c2bb02d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14÷9÷4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544528} {:answer/id #uuid "ec537eaa-e19f-5d5c-86dd-b21e632e6329" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{14}^{4}÷{9}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544599}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec537eaa-e19f-5d5c-86dd-b21e632e6329" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{14}^{4}÷{9}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544599} :answer-field/id #uuid "69de6bef-2216-5a92-af10-7d0e02b6d146" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544702}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9d8ff61d-9e20-514e-9557-6082e8e48970" :question/math-academy-id "q-100726" :question/problem "$(14÷9)^{4}=$" :question/worked-solution "To raise the quotient of two numbers to the power of $4$, we must raise each number to the power of $4,$ and then divide:\n\n$$\n(14÷9)^{4}={14}^{4}÷{9}^{4}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544886 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064279 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bb1eda0-9577-59e7-9c6c-b85966fbb7e4" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Square Root of a Perfect Square Using Absolute Value"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "119c39fb-efd6-5727-8c3b-adac3f25cf28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm 8p" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544123} {:answer/id #uuid "12ee5dfc-4b15-5556-8d87-0dc7c0550e16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16p" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544127} {:answer/id #uuid "8dfd5123-ebf8-5f90-b7bf-84747508777c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8|p|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544395} {:answer/id #uuid "b530d38a-61b2-52e5-8650-d0248f7c86d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16|p|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544468} {:answer/id #uuid "e2ead643-fa73-5935-bee6-e90b2db5ffe5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8p" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544575}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8dfd5123-ebf8-5f90-b7bf-84747508777c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8|p|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544395} :answer-field/id #uuid "30d23bc3-d1e0-5d77-9eab-3d40deacb024" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544672}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e0efe9ed-909f-5452-9389-702e59cbbd2e" :question/math-academy-id "q-129415" :question/problem "$\\sqrt{64{p}^{2}}=$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the square root of a perfect square can be simplified using absolute value.\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{x}^{2}}=|x|\n$$\n\nAlso, we notice that $64$ and ${p}^{2}$ are both perfect squares.\n\nFirst, we write the expression under the square root as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{64{p}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{{8}^{2}\\cdot {p}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8\\cdot 8\\cdot p\\cdot p} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8\\cdot p\\cdot 8\\cdot p} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8p\\cdot 8p} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(8p)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we take the absolute value of the squared quantity and simplify using the rules of absolute value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{(8p)^{2}} & =|8p| \\\\  & =|8|\\cdot |p| \\\\  & =8|p|\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544873 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138512 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dec4e22a-5882-56d9-8e13-b18b4d22efdc" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Distributive Law With Fractions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46fbea6e-db08-56e8-bb91-369d04b61064" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544243}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46fbea6e-db08-56e8-bb91-369d04b61064" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544243} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d17b10b-6d47-53dc-b1b5-ffcdef725bf6" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544684} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8dee5b3b-9724-5b7d-a75e-60c0dc54c024" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544394}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8dee5b3b-9724-5b7d-a75e-60c0dc54c024" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544394} :answer-field/id #uuid "707c01e6-4853-5c0d-b91b-0196a914ed99" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544706}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bdb96e93-5d4a-5d24-ab91-43e3876a1cf8" :question/math-academy-id "q-175481" :question/problem "$\\frac{1}{3}(9x+12y)=${{field-1}}\n$x\\,+$\n{{field-2}}\n$y$" :question/worked-solution "Applying the distributive law and simplifying, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}(9x+12y) & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 9x+\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 12y & = \\\\ \\frac{9x}{3}+\\frac{12y}{3} & = \\\\ 3\\,x+4\\,y & \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544833 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054835 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b765f7b2-639a-5519-9170-02a4fcb0f8fe" :knowledge-point/title "Cases Where the Variable Is on the Right-Hand Side"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "077da333-cb04-5b90-b89f-4cc665733d05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544107}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "077da333-cb04-5b90-b89f-4cc665733d05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544107} :answer-field/id #uuid "883868e4-aa29-5d95-bf5d-f1c4c5b5efca" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544717}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7841e502-bd87-5f16-9f68-b66396ece51e" :question/math-academy-id "q-177595" :question/problem "If $-3=\\frac{a}{9},$ then $a=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's swap the left and right-hand sides so that the variable is on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{9}=-3\n$$\n\nIn this equation, $a$ is being divided by $9.$ To isolate $a$, we can perform the opposite operation, which is *multiplying* by $9.$ So, we multiply both sides of the equation by $9$ and simplify.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{a}{9} & =-3 \\\\ 9\\cdot \\frac{a}{9} & =9\\cdot (-3) \\\\ \\frac{9a}{9} & =-27 \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{9}a}{\\cancel{9}} & =-27 \\\\ a & =-27\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064577 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064247 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f6a7917-f877-5263-abea-a78c0707d9fc" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring Trinomial With Positive Coefficients"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07a9d02b-b538-5620-b733-61af6ed20e19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544108}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07a9d02b-b538-5620-b733-61af6ed20e19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544108} :answer-field/id #uuid "fde59380-e255-5942-b3f7-64c440563de2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061644} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dda4f1f8-6966-5ad9-a7a5-567211e5a9e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544566}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dda4f1f8-6966-5ad9-a7a5-567211e5a9e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544566} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6cbc592-2c25-5ac2-98cd-00aabe81a7d0" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544739} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a3001dc-c422-5f38-bf1a-6e6aac3baf6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544141}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a3001dc-c422-5f38-bf1a-6e6aac3baf6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544141} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed63e6cb-39c3-5d3f-a8c6-4ef7473abfa3" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544769}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e011b39f-8f95-5783-bae7-e146d27b9c74" :question/math-academy-id "q-213910" :question/problem "Factor the following quadratic expression.\n\n$2{x}^{2}+7x+6=(x+\\,$\n{{field-1}}$)($\n{{field-2}}$x\\,+$\n{{field-3}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "We must factor the quadratic expression\n\n$$\n2{x}^{2}+7x+6.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find two numbers that \n\n- multiply to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant ($2\\times 6=12$), and\n\n- add to the linear coefficient ($7$).\n\nSince all coefficients are positive, we only need to consider the positive factor pairs of $12.$\n\nThe positive factor pairs of $12$ are\n\n$$\n(1,12),\\,(2,6),\\,(3,4).\n$$\n\nNotice the numbers in the factor pair $(3,4)$ add to $7:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3+4 & =7\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we write the linear term as $3x+4x,$ and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+7x+6 & = \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+3x+4x+6 & = \\\\ (2{x}^{2}+3x)+(4x+6) & = \\\\ x(2x+3)+2(2x+3) & = \\\\ (x+2)(2x+3) & \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064748 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064298 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "577ff555-4622-5388-8bc3-b49fa39c5674" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Quotient Rule for Exponents"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34071898-2b9d-548e-be48-423897f0a5a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062549} {:answer/id #uuid "2780d2db-b5d8-51a7-86fb-49f658cee304" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{z}^{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544176} {:answer/id #uuid "730b8465-7159-5dad-8c03-b3cb57fc7371" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{z}^{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544344} {:answer/id #uuid "8f5959f9-581b-5aad-9de2-fedc7cc48d68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{z}^{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544399} {:answer/id #uuid "e65f7a1f-1218-5ac9-9a4e-4255893b3a77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544582}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2780d2db-b5d8-51a7-86fb-49f658cee304" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{z}^{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544176} :answer-field/id #uuid "a21dced6-1978-5aed-851e-0ce358a8924e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062545}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "842ce2e6-4b5f-56db-b8c0-d7317ee65b28" :question/math-academy-id "q-325" :question/problem "$\\frac{{z}^{18}}{{z}^{7}}=$" :question/worked-solution "We subtract the exponents, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{z}^{18}}{{z}^{7}} & = \\\\ {z}^{(18-7)} & = \\\\ {z}^{11} & \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544809 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064215 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c0f2a4f3-5117-5796-b35c-524c1e0514cb" :knowledge-point/title "Cases Containing Two Negative Signs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c38baae-067b-54f2-9239-4872cc86443c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4{z}^{6}+2{z}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544221} {:answer/id #uuid "728e061d-9d33-5b2a-a71d-aa061c66944c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4{z}^{5}+2{z}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544342} {:answer/id #uuid "7e43acf4-e376-5667-81ea-3ec76bdc9f74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{z}^{2}+2{z}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544361} {:answer/id #uuid "8ea9c766-9b05-5b66-9fac-dab3d487277e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4{z}^{6}+2{z}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544397} {:answer/id #uuid "d208d9ba-b205-519b-92a8-913080237dda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4{z}^{5}-2z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544531}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "728e061d-9d33-5b2a-a71d-aa061c66944c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4{z}^{5}+2{z}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544342} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9aab190-eecc-5ce2-93c8-fa4f40aa1915" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544731}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3eaa9b96-57f5-5bea-bcf0-0afa03bea744" :question/math-academy-id "q-52181" :question/problem "$-{z}^{3}(4{z}^{2}-2z)=$" :question/worked-solution "We multiply each term inside the parentheses by the term outside the parentheses, simplify using the rules of exponents, and then we add the results together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{z}^{3}(4{z}^{2}-2z) & = \\\\ (-{z}^{3})(4{z}^{2})+(-{z}^{3})(-2z) & = \\\\ ((-1)\\cdot 4)({z}^{3}\\cdot {z}^{2})+((-1)\\cdot (-2))({z}^{3}\\cdot z) & = \\\\ (-4){z}^{3+2}+(2){z}^{3+1} & = \\\\ -4{z}^{5}+2{z}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544874 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073895 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e3287b0-2ecb-5d83-9596-7943d68f8884" :knowledge-point/title "Filling a Ratio Table by Multiplying: Missing Numerators"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b01fea7-f7fa-510d-ada5-b82cd59c7c97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544213} {:answer/id #uuid "6b4bca87-c481-5fd5-9cce-cf8c4bd1e0ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544323} {:answer/id #uuid "dd412dbe-d361-5562-a98f-07652bbbe12d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544565} {:answer/id #uuid "e48f2efa-9d00-5726-a013-c72aec000ba8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544579} {:answer/id #uuid "ed7182da-9af1-58b7-982b-7defc37824ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544600}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e48f2efa-9d00-5726-a013-c72aec000ba8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544579} :answer-field/id #uuid "54429781-1dbd-5d47-83f8-be45001382bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544690}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c2d93ca7-606b-5dec-9244-b98215ef5117" :question/math-academy-id "q-59913" :question/problem "| $5$ | $10$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $?$ | $30$ |\n| $25$ | $50$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nWhat number is missing from the ratio table?" :question/worked-solution "We start with the ratio $5:10$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{10}\n$$\n\nSo the numerator represents the left-hand entry ($5$), and the denominator represents the right-hand entry ($10$).\n\nWe can make a denominator of $30$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5\\times 3}{10\\times 3} & =\\frac{15}{30}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our ratio table:\n\n| $5$ | $10$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $15$ | $30$ |\n| $25$ | $50$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nSo, the missing number is $15.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544801 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068785 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c472f18-da99-5969-acdf-1a7b53465494" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Values that Satisfy a Given Inequality with Fractions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "72626c0b-5f5c-5cb9-adbc-0bef8c4e0cc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544340} {:answer/id #uuid "80055ee4-2037-5288-9370-a3d803b23025" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544367} {:answer/id #uuid "8f7fb084-59b6-501f-b630-33b778bc9852" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544401} {:answer/id #uuid "97d05aa0-8bf6-5cca-b33d-33d9fca2ffa2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544416} {:answer/id #uuid "d21236ed-c80c-5459-a7c5-05b147e79edd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544533}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97d05aa0-8bf6-5cca-b33d-33d9fca2ffa2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544416} :answer-field/id #uuid "312e7e5a-ba75-54c4-a217-c2cd5f4576d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544673}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3861e4f2-43a8-5a8e-aa2e-58c7272cde17" :question/math-academy-id "q-73781" :question/problem "Which of the following values of $w$ satisfy the inequality $\\frac{w}{4}<5\\,?$\n\nI. $w=24$\n\nII. $w=20$\n\nIII. $w=8$" :question/worked-solution "We substitute each value into the given inequality and check to see if the resulting statement is true or false.\n\n- We substitute the value $w=24$ into the given inequality. We get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{w}{4} & <5 \\\\ \\frac{24}{4} & <5 \\\\ 6 & <5\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting $w=24$ into the inequality leads to a **false** statement. \n\nTherefore, $w=24$ **does not satisfy** this inequality.\n\n- We substitute the value $w=20$ into the given inequality. We get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{w}{4} & <5 \\\\ \\frac{20}{4} & <5 \\\\ 5 & <5\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting $w=20$ into the inequality leads to a **false** statement. \n\nTherefore, $w=20$ **does not satisfy** this inequality.\n\n- We substitute the value $w=8$ into the given inequality. We get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{w}{4} & <5 \\\\ \\frac{8}{4} & <5 \\\\ 2 & <5\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting $w=8$ into the inequality leads to a **true** statement. \n\nTherefore, $w=8$ **satisfies** this inequality."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544826 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068702 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2b5f42de-fad6-54b6-90e1-205fdbe8a456" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Multivariable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{